content.text:
**Yes, you can do BMW and use your tech and research skills fully. Brutally honestly: it’s a high-upside, high-execution-risk choice.**

Your research strength fits the core problem: sift through messy customer, market, competitor, and engineering inputs, then explain which requirements matter and why. Your Codex skills can help turn that into a working decision tool. But **Codex is an accelerator, not a substitute for engineering judgment**: if the team can’t validate the data, make the trade-offs coherent, and deliver a reliable demo, research alone won’t win.

Build a product decision system that:

- traces each recommendation to its evidence;
- ranks requirements against cost, feasibility, market demand, and driving enjoyment;
- shows what changes when assumptions change;
- records why a decision was made, so teams can revisit it later.

That plays to your strengths and maps closely to BMW’s brief. A previous BMW Motorrad EHL winner’s judge said the team won despite lacking motorcycle experience because they did strong customer analysis, built a working route algorithm, and pitched its unique value clearly. That is a useful signal, though it’s a different BMW challenge and doesn’t predict this one. [Judge’s account](https://ch.linkedin.com/in/maximilianfreund9)

My honest recommendation: **choose BMW if your team has at least one person who can ship and validate the prototype under time pressure.** If not, the breadth of BMW’s problem makes it riskier than Atira’s more tightly scored task.

